::: {.doc-subject}

Find all bridge edges in the graph.
:::

::: {.doc-usage-source}
Usage

[Source](https://github.com/rich-iannone/edgecase/blob/main/python/edgecase/graph.py#L78248-L78377){target="_blank" rel="noopener"}
:::

::: {.doc-signature .doc-function}
```pythonsignature
Graph.bridges()
```
:::

::: {.doc-text}

![Bridges](../assets/graphs/bridges.light.svg){.lightbox fig-alt="Two triangles joined by the path C, D, E. The edges C–D and D–E are in the accent color: each is the only link between its two sides, so removing it disconnects the graph. The card lists the bridges C–D and D–E." width="100%"}

A bridge (also called a cut edge) is an edge whose removal increases the number of connected
components. Bridges are critical edges that, if removed, would disconnect parts of the
network.

Uses Tarjan's algorithm for undirected graphs (`O(V + E)` time complexity) and strongly
connected component analysis for directed graphs.
:::

## Returns {.doc-returns}

::: {.doc-definition-items}
<code> [list[int]]{.doc-parameter-annotation}</code>

:   List of edge IDs that are bridges. Empty list if no bridges exist.
:::

## Technical Notes {.doc-technical-notes}

Bridge detection uses Tarjan's DFS-based algorithm on undirected graphs, which finds
every bridge in $O(V + E)$ by tracking each node's DFS entry time and the lowest
such time reachable from its subtree; an edge $(u, v)$ is a bridge iff no back edge
from $v$'s subtree reaches $u$ or an earlier ancestor. On directed graphs the notion
generalizes to strongly-connected-component analysis, since removing an edge that
connects two SCCs severs any cross-component paths. Every edge of a tree is a
bridge; a cycle contains none. Bridges are the edge-level analogue of
`articulation_points()`, and together the two identify a graph's most fragile
choke points.

## See Also {.doc-see-also}

[](`~has_bridges`) 

:   Check whether there is any bridge at all.

[](`~local_bridges`) 

:   The edges on no triangle, bridges among them.

[](`~articulation_points`) 

:   The nodes whose removal disconnects the graph.

[](`~k_edge_augmentation`) 

:   The edges to add so that no bridge is left.

## Examples {.doc-examples}

A path has no cycles, so removing any single edge splits it in two, and every edge is a
bridge. A cycle with a tail has just one, the edge to the tail:

::: {#33b46341 .cell execution_count=1}
``` {.python .cell-code}
import edgecase as ec

path = ec.Graph().add_path_graph(4)
tailed = ec.Graph.from_edges(
    [("A", "B"), ("B", "C"), ("C", "D"), ("D", "A"), ("D", "tail")]
)

len(path.bridges()), len(tailed.bridges())
```

::: {.cell-output .cell-output-display execution_count=1}
```
(3, 1)
```
:::
:::


The result holds edge IDs, which resolve back to the nodes they join:

::: {#34fe7db1 .cell execution_count=2}
``` {.python .cell-code}
label = tailed.get_label_from_id
[
    [label(n) for n in tailed.get_node_ids_by_edge_id(e)]
    for e in tailed.bridges()
]
```

::: {.cell-output .cell-output-display execution_count=2}
```
[['D', 'tail']]
```
:::
:::


The bridge picked out. Every other edge lies on the cycle, which still holds together
with any one of them gone:

::: {#3c6f1eae .cell execution_count=3}
``` {.python .cell-code}
spots = {
    "A": (1, 2), "B": (2, 1), "C": (1, 0), "D": (0, 1), "tail": (-1, 1)
}
tailed.set_node_attrs(
    "pos", {tailed.get_id_from_label(v): p for v, p in spots.items()}
)

tailed.plot(
    layout="pos", highlight_edges=tailed.bridges(), label_by="label",
    height=240,
)
```

